abstract: >-
  Technician allocation in industrial maintenance is a domain-specific Human
  Resource Allocation Problem (HRAP). Yet most existing approaches treat
  worker skills as static, overlooking how skill development and fatigue
  shape long-term operational performance. We extend HRAP to a sequential
  Reinforcement Learning (RL) setting in which technician knowledge and
  fatigue evolve endogenously through allocation decisions. We introduce
  FactoReal, a simulator that captures these workforce dynamics, and Hybrid
  Token Transformer RL (HTT-RL), a set-based Transformer policy combining
  categorical embeddings with type-specific numerical encodings and
  supporting varying numbers of technicians and machines. We benchmark
  HTT-RL against thirteen baselines (rule-based, multicriteria,
  optimization-based, and RL-based) across five scenarios, from 2
  technicians and 5 machines to 30 technicians and 97 machines. In the
  lifecycle scenario, a career-length stress test that retires the most
  knowledgeable technicians, HTT-RL and its repair-quality fine-tune obtain
  the two best summary scores of the compared policies on a single episode.
  The fine-tune finishes 5.3% more products than the most productive
  baselines, with the fewest disruptions per product; HTT-RL finishes 1.5%
  more with the most knowledgeable fleet, but with more disruptions per
  product than Topsis. Their production-only counterpart produces 16–20%
  less and falls below random assignment. These results show that
  human-centric objectives can jointly improve workforce sustainability and
  long-term operational performance.
keywords:
  - reinforcement learning
  - industrial maintenance
  - technician dispatching
  - state representation learning
  - continuous-feature encoding
  - manufacturing simulation
